correct\": true, \"speedup\": 17.99058392278145, \"ref_ms\": 2.308689920902252, \"sol_ms\": 0.12832768134772776, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 22.100341796875}, {\"uuid\": \"f77df5ce21634f7ca0586af7e3c13434\", \"num_tokens\": 2, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 17.905231685034742, \"ref_ms\": 2.28490238904953, \"sol_ms\": 0.12761088095605375, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 1.1764403581619263}, {\"uuid\": \"564007ac354e4662a62cc4d6352dc494\", \"num_tokens\": 8, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 34.05160072085313, \"ref_ms\": 4.345006074905395, \"sol_ms\": 0.12760064087808132, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 20.17912483215332}], \"all_correct\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 25.445787707567764, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 26.521503310747153, \"decision\": \"keep_as_best\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 2, \"skill_usage\": [\"none\"], \"notes\": \"First valid/green candidate. Confirms H1: naive-but-correct Triton kernel touching only selected bf16 rows crushes the fp32-materializing reference (17.9x-34.3x). Key observation: sol_ms is ~flat at 0.128ms across num_tokens 2->8 while ref scales with num_tokens, so speedup grows with num_tokens purely because the reference gets slower. Flat 0.128ms => kernel is launch/latency/occupancy-bound (only num_tokens*8 = 16-64 CTAs on 108 SMs), NOT throughput-bound. Next lever (Phase 1, H2/H3): increase num_kv_splits to fill SMs and shrink sol_ms. c003 = num_kv_splits=32.\"}"," {\"candidate_id\": \"c003\", \"candidate\": \"c003\", \"parent\": null, \"source_sha256\": \"9c6a23e5d02c03ddffbe64801be4215414a146cb675b75596ef24e85ef22440e\", \"phase\": \"reconciliation\", \"hypothesis\": \"(operator reconciliation: the original session ended before appending evidence)\", \"change_from_parent\": null, \"workloads\": [{\"uuid\": \"02d6ae9c64ab42ff93f05c23c53bcb7d\", \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": 34.31273763575164}, {\"uuid\": \"ddfa9e340b264f76abe7418692faa876\", \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": 28.15538572286997}, {\"uuid\": \"9d4a5f21268e484ea05a2f2af91d9fa7\", \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": 17.844909951955696}, {\"uuid\": \"f77df5ce21634f7ca0586af7e3c13434\", \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": 17.809348774068308}, {\"uuid\": \"564007ac354e4662a62cc4d6352dc494\", \"correct\": null, \"speedup\": 33.13008319584504}], \"all_correct\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 25.2046316848182, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 26.250493056098133, \"decision\": \"operator-reconciled (pending review)\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 3, \"notes\": \"auto-appended by reconcile_ledger.py at 2026-09-18T05:02:41.626882+08:00 from runs/candidates/c003/feedback.json (feedback_returncode=0)\", \"skills_used\": [], \"static_checks\": [], \"reconciled_by\": \"reconcile_ledger.py\"}","+{\"id\": \"c004\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T05:20:00Z\", \"source_sha256\": \"8cf8c1ed52233f4524b8b1fb1c53b7dcc68f85a85463dc9879a70d5683e88c4e\", \"design\": \"fused_singlepass\", \"params\": {\"BLOCK_N\": 64, \"num_kv_splits\": 1, \"num_warps\": 4, \"num_stages\": 2, \"pv_dtype\": \"bf16\", \"tokens_per_cta\": 1}, \"hypothesis\": \"H6: c002 (splits=8, 25.45x) and c003 (splits=32, 25.20x) both showed sol_ms FLAT at ~0.128ms independent of split count and num_tokens, falsifying the occupancy hypothesis (H2/H3). The ~0.128ms is fixed per-call overhead = two kernel launches + three fp32 scratch allocs (acc/m/l partials). Removing that overhead via a single fused kernel (one launch, no scratch, no combine) should reduce sol_ms.\", \"prechecks\": {\"lse_formula\": true, \"empty_row_guard\": true, \"splitk_combine\": \"n/a_single_pass\", \"tf32_disabled_where_needed\": true, \"int64_gather\": true, \"smem_budget_ok\": true}, \"results\": [{\"uuid\": \"02d6ae9c64ab42ff93f05c23c53bcb7d\", \"num_tokens\": 8, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 22.429169000241675, \"ref_ms\": 4.336947197914124, \"sol_ms\": 0.19336192071437835, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 19.891929626464844}, {\"uuid\": \"ddfa9e340b264f76abe7418692faa876\", \"num_tokens\": 6, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 19.196448989446722, \"ref_ms\": 3.620259847640991, \"sol_ms\": 0.18859007984399795, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 26.15641212463379}, {\"uuid\": \"9d4a5f21268e484ea05a2f2af91d9fa7\", \"num_tokens\": 2, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 12.979526383940746, \"ref_ms\": 2.2851276707649233, \"sol_ms\": 0.17605632156133652, \"max_abs\": 0.0078125, \"max_rel\": 6.687121868133545}, {\"uuid\": \"f77df5ce21634f7ca0586af7e3c13434\", \"num_tokens\": 2, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 12.967923577593206, \"ref_ms\": 2.2852095770835876, \"sol_ms\": 0.17622016072273256, \"max_abs\": 0.015625, \"max_rel\": 8.577619552612305}, {\"uuid\": \"564007ac354e4662a62cc4d6352dc494\", \"num_tokens\": 8, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 22.32829508868319, \"ref_ms\": 4.281088013648986, \"sol_ms\": 0.1917337618768215, \"max_abs\": 0.0078125, \"max_rel\": 33.8658561706543}], \"all_correct\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 17.450335725326212, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 17.980272607981107, \"decision\": \"reject_regression\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 4, \"skill_usage\": [\"none\"], \"notes\": \"H6 FALSIFIED. Fusing to a single-pass kernel made it SLOWER (geomean 17.45x vs c002 25.45x). sol_ms ROSE to 0.176-0.193ms (from ~0.128ms). So the ~0.128ms floor was NOT launch/scratch overhead; the split-K version was genuinely faster because splitting the 2048-index loop across 8 CTAs hides HBM gather latency better than one CTA serially walking all 2048 indices with a big fp32 acc[16,512] held in registers the whole time. Single-CTA-per-token serializes 32 BLOCK_N iterations and grows the live accumulator's register/latency footprint. CONCLUSION: split-K IS the right structure; the ~0.128ms floor is a real fixed cost (likely the combine launch + a genuine latency floor), not something fusion removes. Revert to split-K family. c002 remains BEST. Next (c005): from c002, reduce the FIXED overhead within split-K by shrinking splits toward the sweet spot AND/OR cutting combine cost -- but since 8->32 splits was flat/slightly worse, try splits=4 (fewer combine reads, larger per-CTA work) to test whether the floor drops. Alternatively vary num_warps/num_stages on c002 to probe the floor.\"}"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
